# Ditty News Ticker v4 Development Rules

## v4 Code Scope

When working on v4-related tasks (user mentions "v4" in prompts), you should ONLY modify files in these locations:

### v4 Core Files

- `v4/` - All PHP files for v4 functionality
  - `v4/class-ditty-v4-blocks.php`
  - `v4/class-ditty-v4-layout-renderer.php`
  - `v4/class-ditty-v4-renderer.php`
  - `v4/class-ditty-v4-shortcodes.php`
  - `v4/helpers.php`

### v4 Block Source Files

- `assets/src/scripts/v4/` - v4 JavaScript/React source files
- `assets/src/scripts/blocks/ditty-display/` - Ditty Display block
- `assets/src/scripts/blocks/ditty-display-item/` - Ditty Display Item block
- `assets/src/scripts/blocks/ditty-posts-feed/` - Ditty Posts Feed block

### Build Output (Auto-generated)

- `assets/build/scripts/blocks/ditty-display/`
- `assets/build/scripts/blocks/ditty-display-item/`
- `assets/build/scripts/blocks/ditty-posts-feed/`

## Exceptions: When to Modify Existing Code

You may ONLY modify existing code outside the v4 scope for:

1. **Block Registration** - Adding new block registration in main plugin files
2. **File Includes** - Adding `require_once` or `include` statements to load new v4 files
3. **Hook Registration** - Registering v4 hooks or filters in plugin initialization

## Important Guidelines

- ❌ DO NOT modify legacy code unless registering/including v4 functionality
- ❌ DO NOT modify existing features or functionality
- ✅ DO keep all v4 code isolated in the designated directories
- ✅ DO ensure v4 code is self-contained and doesn't break existing functionality
- ✅ DO follow WordPress coding standards for PHP files
- ✅ DO follow React/JavaScript best practices for block files

## File Structure Pattern

When creating new v4 files:

- PHP classes: `v4/class-ditty-v4-{feature-name}.php`
- Helper functions: `v4/helpers.php` or `v4/helpers-{specific}.php`
- Block source: `assets/src/blocks/{block-name}/`
- Block assets: Follow WordPress block.json schema

## WordPress Block Development

For block files in `assets/src/blocks/`:

- Each block has its own directory with `block.json`, `edit.js`, `save.js`, `render.php`
- Use `@wordpress/blocks` and `@wordpress/block-editor` packages
- Follow WordPress Block API v2+ patterns
- Keep server-side rendering in `render.php` when dynamic output is needed
